WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.810
Speaking, by the way, of understanding math,
I predicted this would happen and it's happening.

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:12.059
We did a video a couple months ago, six weeks
ago called You're About to Get Slammed with

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:13.779
the garbage math.

00:00:13.779 --> 00:00:21.000
And in that video, I made the case that after
these massive declines in the stock market,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:26.860
in employment, job loss, consumer spending,
all of this stuff because of corona virus,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:32.410
at some point from this insane bottom, you
would start to see a recovery.

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:34.989
And percentage wise, the recovery might look
good.

00:00:34.989 --> 00:00:40.379
So you would start to see headlines like and
we have air travel up 120 percent.

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:41.969
Oh, my God.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:42.969
Hundred and twenty percent.

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:43.969
Well, hold on.

00:00:43.969 --> 00:00:48.519
It was down 96 percent to only four percent
of what it was when it goes up.

00:00:48.519 --> 00:00:49.820
One hundred and twenty percent.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.859
It's up to nine percent of what it was.

00:00:51.859 --> 00:00:55.359
Let's not go crazy, but this is the type of
math that they are going to use to trick you.

00:00:55.359 --> 00:00:57.809
And I said it's going to happen with stocks.

00:00:57.809 --> 00:00:59.460
It's going to happen with jobs.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.660
And we are seeing it.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.020
Last week, headlines four point eight million
jobs created.

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.930
Remember, we lost 46 million jobs.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:11.430
Four point eight million came back.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:18.500
Donald Trump went on a national bragging tour
about this is the fastest and best job creation

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.090
in the history of the United States.

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.940
Well, hold on a second.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:27.990
First of all, we got four million jobs back
after losing 46 million.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.560
Number two, what did Trump do to actually
get those jobs back?

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:37.820
We would have way more jobs back had we had
a competent federal response to corona virus

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:39.860
rather than what we experienced.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.350
Again, bragging.

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.830
Unemployment rate down to 11 percent.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:48.360
This is the biggest one month drop in unemployment
in history.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.890
Hold on a second.

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:55.160
We had unemployment of 16, 17 percent, real
unemployment arguably 20 percent.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:02:00.490
You're bragging now that we still have historically
high unemployment.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.560
And they are doing the full garbage math treatment.

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.020
Here's Donald Trump himself just a few days
ago.

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:11.840
The United States economy added almost five
million jobs in the month of June, shattering

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.980
all expectations.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.319
I was watching this morning and the expectations
were much lower than that.

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:24.189
The stock market is doing extremely well,
which means to me jobs, what it means, jobs,

00:02:24.189 --> 00:02:30.610
the largest monthly jobs gain in the history
of our country.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:37.330
The unemployment rate fell by more than two
percentage points, down to just about 11 percent.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.980
We're down to the 11 percent number.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.280
We started at a number of very much higher
than that.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:46.709
As you know, we broke the record last month
and we broke it again this month and an even

00:02:46.709 --> 00:02:47.750
bigger way.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:54.239
This news comes on top of May's extraordinary
jobs report, the largest job gain in history.

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.430
Well, it sounds like things are going pretty
well, right?

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.840
Oh, wait a second.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.569
We lost 46 million jobs before gaining four
point eight million jobs.

00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.329
Now, I think this is a good time to remind
you.

00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:08.180
I want a good economy.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:12.359
I don't want any jobs lost in order to hurt
Donald Trump politically.

00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:16.819
I don't want any corona virus cases or deaths
to hurt Donald Trump politically.

00:03:16.819 --> 00:03:22.610
The reality is, of course, employment is still
well below the pre pandemic level.

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:28.360
We are obviously still in a huge, huge hole
for 15 million fewer jobs in June than in

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:29.400
February.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.779
The unemployment rate is down most recently
from fourteen point seven to eleven point

00:03:34.779 --> 00:03:36.480
one at eleven point one.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.370
It is still higher than at any time since
World War Two.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.980
And again, there's a reporting error that
continues to plague these numbers.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.299
The real number is twelve point one.

00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:51.540
The data from before this data is from before
the spikes that we've seen in Arizona and

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:52.549
Texas and others.

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:56.920
Texas is sort of softening their opening,
telling a lot of people that they should stay

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:57.920
home.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:01.650
So let's see where things look in another
month or two if the pandemic continues to

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:02.650
explode.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:10.480
And, you know, the realistic expectation is
we would see slow improvement to the numbers

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.169
as we get closer to the end of 2020.

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:18.270
And we are in all likelihood, going to start
2021 in a significantly worse position than

00:04:18.270 --> 00:04:19.610
we started 2020.

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:20.610
That's the reality.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.440
So do not fall for the garbage math.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.610
The Eckardt the.

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:29.220
What I think is missing from a lot of these
analysis is the economy can't fully recover

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.030
mathematically until the pandemic is under
control.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:36.570
What I mean by that is until people get all
their earnings back, they don't have the money

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.970
to buy stuff at the rate at which they were
previously.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:42.490
That's just math, that simple math if you
don't have as much money as you did before.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:45.440
You can't spend as much money as you were
spending before.

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.520
Until hospitals can do non-emergency procedures
in full, the medical health care industry

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:51.590
is not going to recover.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:55.680
Now, I think our industry is a complete, immoral
joke.

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.050
The reality is that it's a huge industry that
makes a lot of money until people are going

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:04.590
back to the doctor the way they were before
for elective and non-emergency procedures.

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:10.210
The industry mathematically cannot recover
until then and dine out.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:13.710
Restaurants are fully open and safe.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.070
Restaurants can't recover.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.140
It's just math.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:22.290
If people aren't going back and the capacity
is diminished, the industry cannot recover.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.490
It's just simple arithmetic.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.980
And we've not even started to see the fall
semester at colleges and universities, depending

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:31.710
on how that goes.

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:37.220
You could see massive economic fallout in
the 2021 academic year there.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:38.810
So do not be tricked.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.370
I want things to recover as quickly as possible.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:47.360
But until we actually have the pandemic under
control from a straight up math perspective,

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.390
it cannot happen.

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:48.790
It simply cannot happen.

